-- title:
--   Normal form for a pair cutting out a crossing UV = p^E
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a local ring, and let $p, U, V, X, Y, w \in R$ and $E, E_0 \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume $1 \le E$ and $1 \le E_0$; that $U V = p^{E}$; that the ideals $(p, U)$ and $(p, V)$, each generated by the corresponding pair of elements, are prime; that $U \notin (p, V)$ and $V \notin (p, U)$; that $w$ is a unit and $X Y = p^{E_0} w$; and that there exist units $\gamma$ with $X - \gamma U \in (p, V)$ and (for a possibly different unit) $Y - \gamma V \in (p, U)$, i.e. $X$ is congruent to a unit multiple of $U$ modulo $(p,V)$ and $Y$ is congruent to a unit multiple of $V$ modulo $(p,U)$. Then $E_0 = E$, and there exist $a, b \in R$, both units, with $X = U a$ and $Y = V b$.
--
--   This is the elementary normal-form step for a pair of elements cutting out the two branches of a crossing $UV = p^{E}$ in a local domain: any factorisation $XY = p^{E_0}w$ whose factors match the branches modulo the respective branch primes has the same exponent and differs from $(U,V)$ only by units. It is used in the analysis of the local structure of $X_0(Nq)$ at a supersingular point, where the two branches of the node are compared with an explicit crossing model.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_IsLocalRing_eq_and_exists_isUnit_and_eq_mul_of_mul_eq_pow_of_span_pair_isPrime.lean

